WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.780
- In this video and the one that follows,

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:05.190
I'm going to give you a brief overview

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:08.770
of citizenship rights in early America.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:11.180
Who was considered a citizen?

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.900
Did having citizenship mean
that you had the right to vote?

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.260
How did citizenship and voting
rights change over time?

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.980
So let's go back to the beginning,

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.350
when the U.S. Constitution
took effect in 1789.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.020
Shortly thereafter,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.140
Congress passed the first

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.770
Federal Naturalization Act of 1790.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.790
Remember that naturalization

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.993
is the process by which
someone becomes a citizen.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.160
That act said,

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.550
that any free white
person of good character

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.410
could be a U.S. citizen.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.350
They had to live in the
United States for two years,

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.330
later they changed it to five years,

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:55.100
and in the state where they
applied for citizenship,

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.880
for at least one year,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.370
and then take an oath to
support the Constitution.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:04.700
Who did that include
and who did it exclude?

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:08.570
Any white man who wasn't
an indentured servant,

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.360
meaning someone who had sold

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.210
a number of years of their labor

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.700
for passage to the United States,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.070
or convicted of a crime,

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.700
was eligible to become a citizen

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.300
and therefore enjoy all of the protections

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.100
listed in the Constitution
and Bill of Rights.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.230
Single or widowed white women

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.380
could become citizens on their own.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.930
Married white women and the
children of naturalized citizens

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:38.400
also inherited citizenship from
their husbands and fathers.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.210
This was an era when women and children

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:45.310
were considered the property
of the male head of household,

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.350
so their identity as
citizens was tied to his.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.190
But enjoying all the protections

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.960
listed in the Constitution
and Bill of Rights

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.510
didn't necessarily mean
having the right to vote,

00:01:58.510 --> 00:01:59.920
because the right to vote

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.950
was not listed in the Constitution.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.280
Having U.S. citizenship

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.950
didn't automatically
mean that you could vote.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.280
States control elections,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.890
not the Federal Government,

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:11.760
and most states at this time

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.410
had property requirements for voting.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.090
That means that to vote,

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:21.090
a white man had to have a
certain amount of money or land.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.550
This was based on the idea

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:26.510
that only people who owned
a stake in the community

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.140
should be able to vote,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.790
so this limited the vote
to wealthier white men.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:34.050
White women couldn't vote,

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.540
even though they could be citizens.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.900
The Naturalization Act of 1790

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.370
excluded a lot of people who
lived in the United States

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.000
from being eligible for citizenship.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.720
Indigenous people were not
eligible to become citizens.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.850
People of African descent,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.480
both those who were enslaved
and those who were free,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.730
were not eligible to become citizens,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:00.010
although some states did
permit free black men

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.660
to own property and vote

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.230
if they met those property requirements.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.430
That shows us another
example of how voting rights

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.780
and citizenship were not
necessarily intertwined.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.580
However, over the course
of the next 40 years or so,

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:19.000
whiteness became more and more synonymous

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.470
with voting rights in the United States.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:22.800
By the 1830s,

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.100
almost all states had removed

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.410
their property requirements for voting

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:31.030
and instead permitted any free white man

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.490
over the age of 21 to vote.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:37.160
And as state legislatures
rewrote these laws,

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:40.950
the states that had permitted
property to black men to vote,

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.560
now outlawed them from doing so.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.630
Now it's worth noting that at that time,

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.470
a larger percentage of
the population could vote

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:54.040
in the United States than in
anywhere else in the world,

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.310
but voting became strictly
the domain of white men.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:02.450
That's a quick overview
of the first few decades

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:05.900
of citizenship and voting
rights in early America.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:06.980
In the next video,

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.660
we'll talk about how those rights evolved

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.853
from the 1840s through the 1870s.

